# Great cormorant

The great cormorant is a large, dark seabird of coasts, estuaries and inland
waters across Europe, Asia, Africa, Australasia and eastern North America. It
feeds by descending from the surface and pursuing eels, flatfish and sprats
along the bottom, steering with the tail and driving with fully webbed feet. A
descent typically lasts twenty to forty seconds and ends where it began, the
prey brought up and manipulated head-first before being swallowed. The wettable
plumage that makes the descent efficient must be dried afterwards, which is why
a bird stands with its wings held open. In parts of east Asia the species was
long kept and released on a line to bring back the day's take. See
[[phalacrocoracidae]].
